1
00:00:00,410 --> 00:00:03,060
Have you ever looked back on

2
00:00:03,060 --> 00:00:05,880
an event in your life and
wished it went differently?

3
00:00:05,880 --> 00:00:08,745
Well, we can't help
you go back in time,

4
00:00:08,745 --> 00:00:11,190
but there are things you
can do to ensure you

5
00:00:11,190 --> 00:00:14,235
don't experience the
same missteps again.

6
00:00:14,235 --> 00:00:16,035
In the last video,

7
00:00:16,035 --> 00:00:18,675
we discussed continuous
improvements.

8
00:00:18,675 --> 00:00:20,160
One way to ensure

9
00:00:20,160 --> 00:00:23,580
continuous improvements is
to conduct a retrospective,

10
00:00:23,580 --> 00:00:26,775
so let's discuss that a
little more in depth.

11
00:00:26,775 --> 00:00:30,135
A retrospective is a
workshop or meeting

12
00:00:30,135 --> 00:00:33,870
that gives project teams time
to reflect on a project.

13
00:00:33,870 --> 00:00:37,230
Retrospectives, sometimes
known as retros,

14
00:00:37,230 --> 00:00:39,195
should happen throughout
the life cycle

15
00:00:39,195 --> 00:00:41,270
of a project, but mostly

16
00:00:41,270 --> 00:00:43,550
are implemented after
major milestones,

17
00:00:43,550 --> 00:00:46,870
or most commonly, after
a project is completed.

18
00:00:46,870 --> 00:00:50,540
Retrospectives give you a
chance to discuss successes

19
00:00:50,540 --> 00:00:51,980
and setbacks that took

20
00:00:51,980 --> 00:00:54,470
place within the
project or phases.

21
00:00:54,470 --> 00:00:56,840
You can think of
them as a form of

22
00:00:56,840 --> 00:00:59,615
process improvement
within your project.

23
00:00:59,615 --> 00:01:02,885
Retrospectives serve
three main purposes:

24
00:01:02,885 --> 00:01:04,940
First, they encourage
team building,

25
00:01:04,940 --> 00:01:07,220
because they allow team
members to understand

26
00:01:07,220 --> 00:01:09,940
different perspectives
within their team.

27
00:01:09,940 --> 00:01:11,840
Second, they facilitate

28
00:01:11,840 --> 00:01:14,525
improved collaboration
on future projects.

29
00:01:14,525 --> 00:01:17,045
Third, they promote
positive changes

30
00:01:17,045 --> 00:01:19,460
in future procedures
and processes.

31
00:01:19,460 --> 00:01:22,465
Let's detail each one.

32
00:01:22,465 --> 00:01:24,360
Retrospectives are great for

33
00:01:24,360 --> 00:01:26,750
team-building since they
enable teams to understand

34
00:01:26,750 --> 00:01:28,520
each other better and facilitate

35
00:01:28,520 --> 00:01:31,790
better collaboration, which
improves productivity.

36
00:01:31,790 --> 00:01:33,890
The emphasis in retrospectives is

37
00:01:33,890 --> 00:01:35,820
on continuous improvement
and change,

38
00:01:35,820 --> 00:01:39,140
instead of recycling old—
and potentially bad—habits,

39
00:01:39,140 --> 00:01:41,350
procedures, and processes.

40
00:01:41,350 --> 00:01:44,045
Retrospectives are
helpful because

41
00:01:44,045 --> 00:01:46,910
even if we plan for
every possible risk,

42
00:01:46,910 --> 00:01:49,625
odds are that something
will sneak up on us.

43
00:01:49,625 --> 00:01:51,380
When something does fall through

44
00:01:51,380 --> 00:01:53,820
the cracks and you need to
reflect on it with the team,

45
00:01:53,820 --> 00:01:56,360
you may want to conduct
a retrospective.

46
00:01:56,360 --> 00:01:59,630
Some additional reasons that
you might want to conduct

47
00:01:59,630 --> 00:02:03,395
a retrospective include missed
deadlines or expectations,

48
00:02:03,395 --> 00:02:06,865
or miscommunications
between stakeholders.

49
00:02:06,865 --> 00:02:09,230
You may also want to hold

50
00:02:09,230 --> 00:02:11,450
a retrospective at
the end of a sprint.

51
00:02:11,450 --> 00:02:13,490
As a reminder, a sprint is

52
00:02:13,490 --> 00:02:16,765
a series of ordered
tasks ending in a goal.

53
00:02:16,765 --> 00:02:18,995
You could also hold
a retrospective

54
00:02:18,995 --> 00:02:21,520
after product launches
and landings.

55
00:02:21,520 --> 00:02:23,509
These are all great opportunities

56
00:02:23,509 --> 00:02:25,055
to record key lessons that

57
00:02:25,055 --> 00:02:26,750
other people might learn from as

58
00:02:26,750 --> 00:02:29,095
they work on their own projects.

59
00:02:29,095 --> 00:02:32,460
Identifying the stumbling
blocks and successes in

60
00:02:32,460 --> 00:02:35,595
a project helps improve
future processes,

61
00:02:35,595 --> 00:02:37,160
but the way you decide to

62
00:02:37,160 --> 00:02:39,245
conduct a retrospective can vary.

63
00:02:39,245 --> 00:02:41,300
There's no exact formula or

64
00:02:41,300 --> 00:02:43,700
template for a productive
retrospective;

65
00:02:43,700 --> 00:02:45,470
the way you choose to structure

66
00:02:45,470 --> 00:02:47,180
your retrospective will depend

67
00:02:47,180 --> 00:02:49,460
on your team and workplace.

68
00:02:49,460 --> 00:02:51,830
You may decide to
conduct a formal,

69
00:02:51,830 --> 00:02:53,750
in-person retrospective
if your team

70
00:02:53,750 --> 00:02:56,210
prefers to debrief
in that setting.

71
00:02:56,210 --> 00:02:58,160
You can incorporate sticky notes,

72
00:02:58,160 --> 00:02:59,720
documents, or any other kind of

73
00:02:59,720 --> 00:03:02,215
physical tools to help
your team debrief.

74
00:03:02,215 --> 00:03:04,820
Or if you find that
your team often

75
00:03:04,820 --> 00:03:07,325
gets off track during
in-person meetings,

76
00:03:07,325 --> 00:03:10,625
you may decide a virtual
or online retrospective

77
00:03:10,625 --> 00:03:12,050
is a better option.

78
00:03:12,050 --> 00:03:13,970
In this case, surveys

79
00:03:13,970 --> 00:03:16,345
might help to get
thoughts organized.

80
00:03:16,345 --> 00:03:19,520
Although there's no one way
to conduct retrospectives,

81
00:03:19,520 --> 00:03:22,310
there are certain best
practices to keep in mind.

82
00:03:22,310 --> 00:03:24,230
As we previously mentioned,

83
00:03:24,230 --> 00:03:26,945
you'll want your retrospectives
to be blameless.

84
00:03:26,945 --> 00:03:28,970
Making sure that everyone

85
00:03:28,970 --> 00:03:30,770
feels comfortable
giving feedback as

86
00:03:30,770 --> 00:03:32,555
candidly as possible will

87
00:03:32,555 --> 00:03:35,485
result in the most
productive retrospective.

88
00:03:35,485 --> 00:03:38,000
To navigate through
awkward situations

89
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:39,170
or sensitive subjects,

90
00:03:39,170 --> 00:03:40,835
it may be necessary to encourage

91
00:03:40,835 --> 00:03:43,550
anonymous or private feedback.

92
00:03:43,550 --> 00:03:46,310
A couple of tactics
a project manager

93
00:03:46,310 --> 00:03:48,470
can use to ensure
the process remains

94
00:03:48,470 --> 00:03:50,870
blameless are changing
perspective and

95
00:03:50,870 --> 00:03:54,050
switching from "you"
language to "we" language.

96
00:03:54,050 --> 00:03:56,060
Changing perspective
means putting

97
00:03:56,060 --> 00:03:58,325
yourself in someone else's shoes.

98
00:03:58,325 --> 00:04:01,520
If your team is quick to blame
the delivery company for

99
00:04:01,520 --> 00:04:02,900
their plants not arriving at

100
00:04:02,900 --> 00:04:04,595
the customer's office on time,

101
00:04:04,595 --> 00:04:06,260
think about the situation from

102
00:04:06,260 --> 00:04:08,335
the delivery company's
perspective.

103
00:04:08,335 --> 00:04:10,565
Was the delivery company's route

104
00:04:10,565 --> 00:04:13,670
optimized and tested
to avoid traffic?

105
00:04:13,670 --> 00:04:15,500
If not, maybe that should have

106
00:04:15,500 --> 00:04:17,860
been a task in your project.

107
00:04:17,860 --> 00:04:21,530
Using "you" language can get
you in trouble because it can

108
00:04:21,530 --> 00:04:23,030
feel like everyone in the room is

109
00:04:23,030 --> 00:04:25,505
judging the person
receiving the blame.

110
00:04:25,505 --> 00:04:28,820
For example, telling your
project's sponsor that

111
00:04:28,820 --> 00:04:31,250
"You didn't make it clear
that we didn't have money

112
00:04:31,250 --> 00:04:33,800
for a contingency
budget when plants die"

113
00:04:33,800 --> 00:04:35,300
is not as productive as saying

114
00:04:35,300 --> 00:04:37,235
"The lack of a contingency budget

115
00:04:37,235 --> 00:04:38,510
wasn't made fully clear from

116
00:04:38,510 --> 00:04:40,190
the get-go, and that's
something that we can

117
00:04:40,190 --> 00:04:42,290
improve upon for next time."

118
00:04:42,290 --> 00:04:45,185
The project sponsor may feel
attacked and wonder why you,

119
00:04:45,185 --> 00:04:47,030
the project manager, didn't ask

120
00:04:47,030 --> 00:04:49,265
the right questions
in the early stages.

121
00:04:49,265 --> 00:04:50,840
Maybe the fact of the matter

122
00:04:50,840 --> 00:04:53,030
is that both of you
could have done a little

123
00:04:53,030 --> 00:04:54,230
bit more to include

124
00:04:54,230 --> 00:04:56,795
a contingency budget,
and that's okay.

125
00:04:56,795 --> 00:04:59,890
Make sure you aren't only
focusing on the negative.

126
00:04:59,890 --> 00:05:02,120
Retrospectives are
about reflecting on

127
00:05:02,120 --> 00:05:05,050
the positive aspects
of projects too,

128
00:05:05,050 --> 00:05:08,790
so talk about what went
well. What was fun?

129
00:05:08,790 --> 00:05:10,850
What new things
will you be able to

130
00:05:10,850 --> 00:05:13,340
carry with you into
future projects?

131
00:05:13,340 --> 00:05:15,170
Maybe the sales and
marketing teams

132
00:05:15,170 --> 00:05:16,325
don't work together often,

133
00:05:16,325 --> 00:05:18,835
but this gave them an
opportunity to bond.

134
00:05:18,835 --> 00:05:20,630
Maybe you enjoyed working with

135
00:05:20,630 --> 00:05:22,190
the Project Plant Pals contracted

136
00:05:22,190 --> 00:05:24,020
website designer so
much that the team

137
00:05:24,020 --> 00:05:26,285
has decided to hire
them full-time.

138
00:05:26,285 --> 00:05:29,600
Whatever the positives were,
they're worth celebrating.

139
00:05:29,600 --> 00:05:31,220
You could even order some dinner

140
00:05:31,220 --> 00:05:33,725
or dessert as a thank
you to everyone.

141
00:05:33,725 --> 00:05:36,545
Finally, you'll want to make sure

142
00:05:36,545 --> 00:05:39,025
that you enact the
changes you've discussed.

143
00:05:39,025 --> 00:05:41,600
You'll put the discussed
changes in place and

144
00:05:41,600 --> 00:05:42,920
decide to handle the project a

145
00:05:42,920 --> 00:05:45,065
little differently
in the next phase.

146
00:05:45,065 --> 00:05:47,150
People don't want
to participate in

147
00:05:47,150 --> 00:05:49,100
retrospectives if they
feel their feedback

148
00:05:49,100 --> 00:05:53,395
isn't fully taken into
consideration and implemented.

149
00:05:53,395 --> 00:05:57,020
Now that you have a general
overview for retrospectives,

150
00:05:57,020 --> 00:05:59,840
we'll build upon this
foundation in the next video,

151
00:05:59,840 --> 00:06:01,700
where we'll demonstrate
how to conduct

152
00:06:01,700 --> 00:06:04,890
your own retrospective.
Meet you there.
